[# Section 7 — bindings: C ABI, two-layer model, type mapping,
   thread safety, lifetime, codegen, quickstarts.
   Anchored in spec/bindings.md + spec/abi.md + spec/misc/parity-matrix.md.
   CX ships V (native reference), Python, Go, Rust as Tier-1 bindings.
   File extension .cxd — data-only source. #]

[section id=bindings group='Ring 3 — Ecosystem' n=7 title='Bindings'

  [intro
    """libcx is a V library. Every other supported language is a
       binding that wraps the compiled C ABI. There are two layers
       per binding: **Layer 1** (the conformance contract — 16
       canonical methods, byte-identical semantics across hosts) and
       **Layer 2** (opt-in host-idiomatic sugar that desugars to
       Layer 1). CX ships V, Python, Go, and Rust at Tier 1; six
       other bindings (TypeScript, Java, C#, Ruby, Kotlin, Swift) are
       archived under `lang/_archived/` pending restoration — see
       [[migration]]. Normative spec:
       [`spec/03-approved/misc/bindings.md`](../../spec/03-approved/misc/bindings.md). C ABI surface:
       [`spec/03-approved/core/abi.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/abi.md). Per-binding gate status:
       [`spec/03-approved/misc/parity-matrix.md`](../../spec/03-approved/misc/parity-matrix.md)."""]

  [child id=c-abi n=7.1 title='The C ABI surface'
    [intro
      """libcx exposes a narrow, stable C ABI. Every binding sees the
         same symbol set; capability bits (`cx_features()`) tell a
         binding what optional features the loaded libcx supports.
         The full ABI surface — including signatures, error channels,
         and lifetime rules — is documented in
         [`spec/03-approved/core/abi.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/abi.md);
         this section is the working summary for binding authors."""]

    [body
      """The ABI is **string-in, string-out**. Nearly every symbol
         takes NUL-terminated UTF-8 text and returns a heap-allocated
         UTF-8 string (or a length-framed binary buffer) that the
         caller releases with `cx_free()`. There is no document handle
         at this layer — no parse-once-then-navigate object, no node
         accessors, no per-document release call. The exceptions are
         the explicit **handle families** — streaming events
         (`cx_events_*`), table readers and writers
         (`cx_table_reader_*` / `cx_table_writer_*`), and the
         streaming event writer (`cx_events_writer_*`) — each with its
         own open / pull-or-emit / close lifecycle. The full symbol
         catalog is printed in [[c-abi-detail]]."""]

    [body
      """The Layer-1 `Doc` / `Node` surface ([[layer-1]]) is a
         **binding-side construct** built on this string ABI: each
         binding parses the document into its own host-language AST,
         answers `Node` accessors by walking that AST, and delegates
         selection, evaluation, and update to `cx_code_eval`.
         Identity — `bytes()` / `hash()` / `equals()` — rides
         `cx_canonical`, `cx_hash`, and `cx_eq`. Nothing about a Doc
         is retained inside libcx between calls."""]

    [child id=lifecycle n=7.1.1 title='Lifecycle — init, threads, features'
      [body
        """Three functions form the binding-to-libcx handshake. They
           are mandatory for every Tier-1 binding and
           cheap-and-harmless for every other binding. Per
           [`spec/03-approved/core/abi.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/abi.md) §1.6."""]
      [list
        [item """`cx_init()` — idempotent. Every binding calls
                it once at module load. Enables host-thread
                registration in libcx's GC. Returns 0."""]
        [item """`cx_thread_register()` — first call from any
                host-spawned worker thread, before any other `cx_*`
                call on that thread. Idempotent (duplicate registration
                returns 0). Returns 0 on success or duplicate; -1 on
                real failure."""]
        [item """`cx_thread_unregister()` — when a worker thread
                exits, the binding unregisters to release the GC's
                stack-bottom record for that thread. Optional but
                recommended for long-lived processes with churn in
                their thread pool."""]
        [item """`cx_features()` — returns a NUL-terminated
                lowercase hex string encoding the capability bitmask.
                Bindings call this once at load and again any time a
                new-since-binding-built code path is taken. See
                [[cap-bit-negotiation]] for the negotiation
                protocol."""]
        [item """`cx_version()` / `cx_abi_version()` —
                return the libcx semver and the ABI semver
                respectively. Bindings include both in their
                version-report output."""]]
      [note
        """Bit 26 of the `cx_features()` bitmask (parsed from the hex
           string) confirms the loaded libcx advertises the
           thread-init handshake. Bindings that need the handshake
           must refuse to load against a libcx that does not set this
           bit. Bindings that don't need it (V, single-threaded
           callers) may ignore the bit."""]]

    [child id=transcoding n=7.1.2 title='Canonical form and identity'
      [body
        """Format conversion and identity are pure string functions.
           Every binding's Layer-1 `Doc.bytes()` / `.hash()` /
           `.equals()` maps directly onto them."""]
      [list
        [item """`cx_to_cx(input, err_out)` — parse CX text and
                return the lossless canonical text. The `cx_to_*` /
                `cx_*_to_*` family covers every input/output format
                pair (CX, XML, JSON, YAML, TOML, plus the AST and
                binary projections)."""]
        [item """`cx_canonical(input, err_out)` — strict canonical
                text: presentation stripped, byte-identical output
                for any data-equivalent inputs."""]
        [item """`cx_hash(input, err_out)` — SHA-256 hex (64 chars)
                of the strict-canonical bytes."""]
        [item """`cx_eq(a, b, err_out)` — returns the string `"1"`
                iff strict-canonical(a) == strict-canonical(b), else
                `"0"`."""]]
      [body
        """All of these are **stateless** (class S in the abi.md
           concurrency classification): concurrent calls on disjoint
           inputs are safe without external synchronization, and
           concurrent calls on the *same* input buffer are also safe
           because the input is never mutated."""]]

    [child id=evaluation n=7.1.3 title='Evaluation — cx_code_eval family'
      [body
        """The `cx_code_eval*` family is the single evaluator
           entry point for code, patterns, paths, and transforms.
           Callers write selection as a path expression or a
           `[?for PATTERN]` program and pass it through
           `cx_code_eval`."""]
      [list
        [item """`cx_code_eval(input, program, output_target, err_out)`
                — evaluate the program against the input document and
                return the result rendered to the target (text, cx,
                json, yaml, xml, csv, tsv). Cap bit 28."""]
        [item """`cx_code_eval_caps(input, program, output_target,
                caps, err_out)` — capability-aware variant (cap bit
                38): `caps` is the deny-by-default host grant spec
                (`""` = pure-only, `"all"` = full grant,
                `"read,net=host:443"` = scoped grants)."""]
        [item """`cx_code_eval_with_len(...)` — explicit-length
                variant for payloads carrying embedded NUL bytes."""]
        [item """`cx_code_eval_streaming(..., write_cb, user, err_out)`
                — deliver rendered output to a callback in chunks.
                Streams incrementally only for streamable program
                shapes; `cx_code_eval_streamable()` reports in advance
                whether a (program, target) pair will stream or
                buffer."""]]
      [body
        """Bindings expose `Doc.eval(code)` as their Layer-1 surface;
           `Doc.select_all(cxpath)` and `Doc.select(cxpath)` desugar
           to a `[?for]` program over the path, passed through
           `cx_code_eval` — the binding pays a small program-build
           cost, the C ABI stays narrow. See [[cxpath]] for the path
           vocabulary."""]]

    [child id=structural n=7.1.4 title='Structural access is binding-side'
      [body
        """There are **no node accessors at the C ABI**. A binding
           obtains the document structure once — its own parser, or
           the `cx_to_ast_bin` binary AST — and answers `Node.name()`
           / `.attr()` / `.attrs()` / `.children()` / `.body()` /
           `.kind()` by walking that host-side tree. This keeps the
           ABI narrow and makes node access a zero-FFI operation
           after the initial parse."""]]

    [child id=modify-abi n=7.1.5 title='Modify — pure-functional updates'
      [body
        """There is no dedicated update symbol either. Layer-1
           `Doc.modify(focus, action)` builds a `[?modify]` program
           from the focus path and the action clause (a CX program
           string such as `[set 'Alice']`) and evaluates it through
           `cx_code_eval`. The result is a new document; the input is
           unchanged."""]]

    [child id=abi-error-channel n=7.1.6 title='Error channel'
      [body
        """Every fallible ABI symbol takes an `err_out` parameter — a
           `char**` that on failure points to a NUL-terminated UTF-8
           message in the wire format `CXERnnnn:msg` (e.g.
           `CXER0100:parse: …`). The binding extracts the code,
           wraps the message in its single host error type, and
           releases the buffer with `cx_free()`."""]
      [table
        [tr [th 'code-range'] [th 'owner'] [th 'registry']]
        [tr [td 'CXER0001–CXER0009'] [td 'Generic-core panic + core-internal failures'] [td 'spec/03-approved/core/code.md §9.4']]
        [tr [td 'CXER0100–CXER0299'] [td 'CX language core (directive errors)'] [td 'spec/03-approved/core/code.md §9.4']]
        [tr [td 'CXERLEX-*'] [td 'Lexical-layer rejects (named suffix, non-numeric)'] [td 'spec/03-approved/formal/lexicon.ebnf']]
        [tr [td 'CXER1100 and up'] [td 'cx-stdlib packs — each pack owns its band'] [td 'spec/03-approved/process/governance.md §9.6']]
        [tr [td 'cx-err:E901–E911'] [td 'Include resolution (parse-time data-parse codes)'] [td 'spec/03-approved/core/cxdm.md §11']]]
      [body
        """Each binding carries the code **verbatim** in one error
           type — Python `CxError(RuntimeError)` with `.code` /
           `.message`, Go `CxError`, Rust `Result<_, String>` with the
           code in the message, V an error string. There is no
           per-family exception hierarchy, and lint findings are not
           errors at all (they are structured diagnostics from
           `cx_lint`). The namespace registry in
           [`spec/03-approved/process/governance.md`](../../spec/03-approved/process/governance.md)
           §9.6 is the single source of truth; codes are stable and
           additions get fresh numbers."""]]]

  [child id=layer-1 n=7.2 title='Layer 1 — canonical method set'
    [intro
      """Layer 1 is the **conformance contract**. Every binding
         implements identical method names (case-adjusted to host
         conventions) with identical semantics. Drift between
         bindings on any Layer-1 method is a release-blocker (gate
         28.6, Layer-1 parity). Normative spec:
         [`spec/03-approved/misc/bindings.md`](../../spec/03-approved/misc/bindings.md) §2."""]

    [body
      """**16 canonical methods.** No more, no less, at this layer.
         The result of every Layer-1 method is byte-identical across
         V / Python / Go / Rust on the same input — a property the
         conformance harness validates by hashing each result and
         comparing per-binding."""]

    [table
      [tr [th 'method'] [th 'returns'] [th 'description'] [th 'implemented-over']]
      [tr [td 'parse(bytes)'] [td 'Doc'] [td 'Parse canonical CX bytes'] [td 'binding-side parser / AST']]
      [tr [td 'Doc.bytes()'] [td 'bytes'] [td 'Serialize Doc to canonical CX'] [td 'cx_canonical']]
      [tr [td 'Doc.hash()'] [td 'string'] [td 'SHA-256 of canonical bytes (hex)'] [td 'cx_hash']]
      [tr [td 'Doc.equals(other)'] [td 'bool'] [td 'Canonical-bytes equality'] [td 'cx_eq / cx_hash']]
      [tr [td 'Doc.eval(code)'] [td 'string'] [td 'Evaluate CX code against doc'] [td 'cx_code_eval']]
      [tr [td 'Doc.select_all(cxpath)'] [td '[Node]'] [td 'CXPath path-value (all matches)'] [td 'cx_code_eval ([?for] program)']]
      [tr [td 'Doc.select(cxpath)'] [td 'Node?'] [td 'First match of select_all'] [td 'cx_code_eval ([?for] program)']]
      [tr [td 'Doc.modify(focus, action)'] [td 'Doc'] [td 'Pure-functional update'] [td 'cx_code_eval ([?modify] program)']]
      [tr [td 'Doc.find_all(name)'] [td '[Node]'] [td 'Name-only convenience'] [td 'binding-side AST walk']]
      [tr [td 'Doc.root()'] [td 'Node'] [td 'Root element'] [td 'binding-side AST walk']]
      [tr [td 'Node.name()'] [td 'string'] [td 'Element name'] [td 'binding-side AST walk']]
      [tr [td 'Node.attr(name)'] [td 'Value?'] [td 'Attribute value'] [td 'binding-side AST walk']]
      [tr [td 'Node.attrs()'] [td 'Map'] [td 'All attributes (preserves order)'] [td 'binding-side AST walk']]
      [tr [td 'Node.children()'] [td '[Node]'] [td 'Direct children'] [td 'binding-side AST walk']]
      [tr [td 'Node.body()'] [td 'Value'] [td 'Element body value'] [td 'binding-side AST walk']]
      [tr [td 'Node.kind()'] [td 'string'] [td 'Item-kind tag'] [td 'binding-side AST walk']]]

    [body
      """**CXPath is the selector vocabulary** across every Layer-1
         selection method. The path string is parsed and evaluated by
         libcx, not by the binding — that keeps semantics identical
         across hosts and lets the evaluator's program cache work
         cross-binding. At the binding surface a CXPath is a **plain
         string** in every language — there is no dedicated path type;
         the string is passed through to the evaluator verbatim."""]

    [body
      """**Per-method semantics — selected highlights:**"""]
    [list
      [item """`parse(bytes)` — accepts CX text and produces a Doc
              (the binding's own parsed AST plus the retained source
              bytes)."""]
      [item """`Doc.hash()` — always returns the strict-canonical
              SHA-256 (per [[canonical-strict]]) via `cx_hash`.
              There is no lossless-canonical hash method; the
              lossless form is a formatting surface (`cx_fmt`), not
              an identity surface."""]
      [item """`Doc.eval(code)` — takes a CX program as a string (a
              path expression, a `[?for ...]` comprehension, any
              program) and returns the rendered result string from
              `cx_code_eval`."""]
      [item """`Doc.select_all(cxpath)` — returns a host-native
              sequence of Nodes. Empty result is the host's empty
              sequence (Python `[]`, Rust `Vec::new()`, Go `nil`
              slice, V `[]Node{}`)."""]
      [item """`Doc.select(cxpath)` — returns the first Node or
              the host's "no value" form (Python `None`, Rust
              `Option::None`, Go `nil` pointer, V
              `?Node`)."""]
      [item """`Doc.modify(focus, action)` — always returns a
              new Doc; the receiver is unchanged. The action is a CX
              program string (e.g. `[set 'Alice']`). If `focus`
              matches nothing, returns an unchanged copy (not an
              error)."""]
      [item """`Doc.find_all(name)` — name-only convenience walk
              over the binding-side AST; equivalent result to
              selecting `//name`."""]
      [item """`Node.attr(name)` — returns the host's "absent"
              form when the attribute is missing (NOT an error).
              Inspect `Node.attrs()` to distinguish
              "absent" from "present-with-null-value"."""]
      [item """`Node.body()` — for scalar bodies, returns the
              typed scalar (int, string, etc.). For mixed-content
              bodies, returns a host sequence of scalars +
              Nodes."""]
      [item """`Node.kind()` — reserved for the item-kind taxonomy
              (element / scalar kinds / collections). The full
              discrimination is **not implemented yet**: the current
              bindings return `'element'` for the element Nodes the
              selection surface produces (the Rust binding hardcodes
              exactly that), and no binding ships a typed kind
              enum."""]]

    [example lang=python
      """# Python — Layer 1
         from cxlib import Doc
         doc = Doc.parse(open("users.cx", "rb").read())
         emails = doc.select_all("//user[= $_@active true]/@email")
         new_doc = doc.modify("//user[= $_@id 1]/@name", "[set 'Alice']")
         assert new_doc.hash() != doc.hash()  # different content
         assert Doc.parse(doc.bytes()).hash() == doc.hash()  # round-trip"""]

    [example lang=go
      """// Go — Layer 1 (PascalCase per Go style; semantics identical)
         doc, _ := cxlib.ParseDoc(data)
         emails, _ := doc.SelectAll("//user[= $_@active true]/@email")
         newDoc, _ := doc.Modify("//user[= $_@id 1]/@name", "[set 'Alice']")
         h1, _ := newDoc.Hash()
         h2, _ := doc.Hash()
         if h1 == h2 { panic("modify was no-op") }"""]

    [example lang=rust
      """// Rust — Layer 1
         let doc = Doc::parse(&data)?;
         let emails = doc.select_all("//user[= $_@active true]/@email")?;
         let new_doc = doc.modify("//user[= $_@id 1]/@name", "[set 'Alice']")?;
         assert_ne!(new_doc.hash()?, doc.hash()?);"""]

    [example lang=v
      """// V — Layer 1 (native — no FFI wrapper)
         doc := cx.parse_doc(data)!
         emails := doc.select_all('//user[= $_@active true]/@email')!
         new_doc := doc.modify('//user[= $_@id 1]/@name', "[set 'Alice']")!
         assert new_doc.hash()! != doc.hash()!"""]

    [note
      """**Byte-identical across bindings (gate 28.6).** For any
         input bytes B and code C, the conformance suite asserts
         `V.Doc.parse(B).eval(C).bytes() == Python.… == Go.… == Rust.…`.
         A binding that drifts on a single byte of output blocks the
         release. The matrix lives in
         [`spec/03-approved/misc/parity-matrix.md`](../../spec/03-approved/misc/parity-matrix.md)."""]]

  [child id=layer-2 n=7.3 title='Layer 2 — host idiom packs'
    [intro
      """Layer 2 is **opt-in sugar**. Importing it pulls in
         host-idiomatic wrappers that compile to Layer-1 calls at the
         boundary. The compilation is deterministic — every Layer-2
         expression has a single, documented Layer-1 desugaring. Spec:
         [`spec/03-approved/misc/bindings.md`](../../spec/03-approved/misc/bindings.md) §3."""]

    [body
      """**Why two layers? Two audiences.** **CX-native developers**
         want CX vocabulary in every host language — they get Layer 1
         and never touch Layer 2. **Host-native developers** want
         list comprehensions, filter chains, iterator combinators,
         derive macros — they get Layer 2 and the Layer-1 surface
         stays available underneath. Either audience can mix the two
         in the same module."""]

    [child id=layer-2-python n=7.3.1 title='Python — cxlib.idioms'
      [body
        """`cxlib.idioms` ships a `Doc` subclass with Pythonic
           dunders over Layer 1: `__getitem__` with a CXPath string
           (→ `select_all`), `__setitem__` that desugars to `modify`
           + `[set …]`, division iteration (`doc / "//path"`),
           `__contains__`, `clone()`, and `explain(expr)` returning
           the Layer-1 equivalent of any Layer-2 expression."""]
      [example lang=python
        """from cxlib.idioms import Doc, explain   # opt-in

           doc = Doc.parse(open("users.cx", "rb").read())

           # Subscript with CXPath string
           active = doc["//user[= $_@active true]"]
           # ≡ doc.select_all("//user[= $_@active true]")

           # Subscript-assignment desugars to modify + [set …]
           new_doc = doc.clone()
           new_doc["//user[= $_@id 1]/@name"] = "Alice"
           # ≡ new_doc.modify("//user[= $_@id 1]/@name", '[set "Alice"]')

           # Division iterator over a path
           for email in doc / "//user/@email":
               print(email)
           # ≡ for email in doc.select_all("//user/@email"):

           # Explain — surface the Layer-1 desugaring
           print(explain(doc / "//user"))
           # -> doc.select_all("//user")"""]
      [body
        """List comprehensions directly over the Doc (and typed
           projections from a schema) are **not built yet** — the
           comprehension path needs CXPath-from-AST inference at the
           host level and is deferred to a later phase per the
           module's own docstring. The subscript / division pair
           covers the common path today."""]]

    [child id=layer-2-go n=7.3.2 title='Go — Layer-2 in package cxlib'
      [body
        """The Go Layer-2 surface lives in the same `cxlib` package
           (there is no separate `idioms` import path): a chainable
           `Filter` / `Where` / `Get` builder that folds into one
           Layer-1 `SelectAll` call, `Set` / `Delete` / `Rename`
           action builders for `Modify`, `MustSelectAll` /
           `MustSelect` panic-on-error variants, and
           `Explain(op, args...)` returning the Layer-1
           desugaring."""]
      [example lang=go
        """import "github.com/cx-home/cx/lang/go/cxlib"

           doc, _ := cxlib.ParseDoc(data)

           // Builder chain → CXPath string under the hood
           emails, _ := doc.Filter("user").Where("= $_@active true").Get("/@email")
           // ≡ doc.SelectAll("//user[= $_@active true]/@email")

           // Action builders — each produces a CX program string
           action, _ := cxlib.Set("Alice")      // "[set \"Alice\"]", error
           newDoc, _ := doc.Modify("//user[= $_@id 1]/@name", action)
           _, _ = doc.Modify("//user[= $_@banned true]", cxlib.Delete())

           // Explain
           s, _ := cxlib.Explain("filter", "user", "= $_@active true")
           fmt.Println(s, len(emails), newDoc.Bytes() != nil)"""]
      [body
        """There are **no struct-tag projections and no `Project[T]`
           generic** — typed materialization into host structs is not
           built. Note the two-value `Set` return: it validates the
           value and hands back `(program string, error)`; the other
           builders are infallible and return the string directly."""]]

    [child id=layer-2-rust n=7.3.3 title='Rust — cxlib::idioms'
      [body
        """`cxlib::idioms` ships the `DocExt` extension trait —
           `select_iter` (iterator form of `select_all`), `find_iter`
           (name-only, no CXPath parse), and `diagram_with(format)` —
           plus `explain(op, arg)` returning the Layer-1
           desugaring."""]
      [example lang=rust
        """use cxlib::code::Doc;
           use cxlib::idioms::{DocExt, explain};

           let doc = Doc::parse(&data)?;

           // Iterator over selection results
           let emails: Vec<_> = doc
               .select_iter("//user[= $_@active true]/@email")?
               .collect();

           // Name-only iteration — no CXPath parse
           let users: Vec<_> = doc.find_iter("user").collect();

           // Explain — the Layer-1 desugaring
           assert_eq!(explain("select_iter", "//user"),
                      r#"doc.select_all("//user")?.into_iter()"#);"""]
      [body
        """There is **no** `#[derive(CxData)]`, **no** `cx!` macro,
           **no** `collect::<T>()` typed projection, and **no
           proc-macro crate** — those surfaces are unbuilt. The
           idioms layer is the iterator/explain trio above;
           everything else is Layer 1."""]]

    [child id=layer-2-v n=7.3.4 title='V — Layer 1 is already idiomatic'
      [body
        """V is the native reference. There is no separate Layer 2
           wrapper because the Layer-1 surface IS V's idiomatic API —
           V naming conventions, V error semantics (`!`/`?`), V
           collection types. Adding a sugar layer would only
           duplicate what's already there."""]
      [body
        """If you find yourself wanting "Layer 2" in V, you are
           describing a feature the V binding should expose at
           Layer 1 in the first place. File an issue against
           `lang/v/native/`."""]]

    [note
      """**Layer-2 idiom packs are not API-conformance-tested.**
         Different bindings have different idioms by design — Python
         subscripts vs Go builder chains vs Rust iterators. The
         conformance harness does test the Layer-1 desugaring of
         every documented Layer-2 expression — `cx.explain(expr)`
         returns the Layer-1 equivalent and is used both in fixtures
         and in LSP hovers. A Layer-2 method that does not desugar
         deterministically to Layer 1 is rejected on review."""]]

  [child id=type-mapping n=7.4 title='Type mapping per binding'
    [intro
      """Every CX scalar kind maps to a specific host type in each
         binding. The mapping is mandatory at Layer 1 (gate 28.4,
         type-mapping conformance); a binding that deviates blocks
         release. Per [`spec/03-approved/misc/bindings.md`](../../spec/03-approved/misc/bindings.md) §4."""]

    [body
      """**Scalar kinds.** The eleven CX atomic kinds (per
         [[scalars]]) plus sized variants flow to host types as
         follows. The leftmost column lists the CX type annotation
         (the `::` glue); each subsequent column gives the host's
         primary representation. Normative source:
         [`spec/03-approved/misc/type-mapping.md`](../../spec/03-approved/misc/type-mapping.md)."""]

    [table
      [tr [th 'cx-type'] [th 'v'] [th 'python'] [th 'go'] [th 'rust']]
      [tr [td '::int'] [td 'i64'] [td 'int'] [td 'int64'] [td 'i64']]
      [tr [td '::i8'] [td 'i8'] [td 'int'] [td 'int8'] [td 'i8']]
      [tr [td '::i16'] [td 'i16'] [td 'int'] [td 'int16'] [td 'i16']]
      [tr [td '::i32'] [td 'i32'] [td 'int'] [td 'int32'] [td 'i32']]
      [tr [td '::i64'] [td 'i64'] [td 'int'] [td 'int64'] [td 'i64']]
      [tr [td '::u8'] [td 'u8'] [td 'int'] [td 'uint8'] [td 'u8']]
      [tr [td '::u16'] [td 'u16'] [td 'int'] [td 'uint16'] [td 'u16']]
      [tr [td '::u32'] [td 'u32'] [td 'int'] [td 'uint32'] [td 'u32']]
      [tr [td '::u64'] [td 'u64'] [td 'int'] [td 'uint64'] [td 'u64']]
      [tr [td '::float'] [td 'f64'] [td 'float'] [td 'float64'] [td 'f64']]
      [tr [td '::f32'] [td 'f32'] [td 'float'] [td 'float32'] [td 'f32']]
      [tr [td '::f64'] [td 'f64'] [td 'float'] [td 'float64'] [td 'f64']]
      [tr [td '::decimal'] [td 'Decimal (custom)'] [td 'decimal.Decimal'] [td 'apd.Decimal (exact decimal — never big.Float)'] [td 'bigdecimal::BigDecimal (not rust_decimal)']]
      [tr [td '::bigint'] [td 'big.Integer'] [td 'int'] [td '*big.Int'] [td 'num_bigint::BigInt']]
      [tr [td '::string'] [td 'string'] [td 'str'] [td 'string'] [td 'String / &str']]
      [tr [td '::bool'] [td 'bool'] [td 'bool'] [td 'bool'] [td 'bool']]
      [tr [td '::date'] [td 'time.Time (date-only)'] [td 'datetime.date'] [td 'time.Time'] [td 'chrono::NaiveDate']]
      [tr [td '::datetime'] [td 'time.Time'] [td 'datetime.datetime (aware)'] [td 'time.Time'] [td 'chrono::DateTime<FixedOffset>']]
      [tr [td '::bytes'] [td '[]u8'] [td 'bytes'] [td '[]byte'] [td 'Vec<u8>']]
      [tr [td 'atom (:name)'] [td 'cx.Atom'] [td 'cxlib.Atom'] [td 'cxlib.Atom'] [td 'cxlib::Atom']]
      [tr [td 'null'] [td 'json2.Null'] [td 'None'] [td 'nil'] [td 'Option::None']]]

    [body
      """**Container kinds.** Sequences, arrays, and maps (see
         [[collections]]) map to host containers preserving the
         container-vs-atom distinction (cxdm §2.0)."""]

    [table
      [tr [th 'cx-container'] [th 'v'] [th 'python'] [th 'go'] [th 'rust']]
      [tr [td 'sequence'] [td '[]Value'] [td 'list[Value]'] [td '[]Value'] [td 'Vec<Value>']]
      [tr [td 'array'] [td 'Array'] [td 'list (nested)'] [td '[]interface{}'] [td 'Array (Vec<Value> newtype)']]
      [tr [td 'map'] [td 'map[ScalarKey]Value'] [td 'dict[ScalarKey, Value]'] [td 'map[interface{}]Value'] [td 'HashMap<ScalarKey, Value>']]
      [tr [td 'table'] [td 'Table'] [td 'cxlib.Table'] [td 'cxlib.Table'] [td 'cxlib::Table']]
      [tr [td 'element'] [td 'Element'] [td 'Element'] [td 'Element'] [td 'Element']]]

    [body
      """**Overflow and edge cases.** Host languages have different
         numeric ranges than CX. Two rules cover the boundary: an
         unsized integer that exceeds i64 **auto-promotes to bigint**
         (never an error), and a value ascribed a sized type it does
         not fit is **rejected at parse time with CXERLEX-RANGE** —
         never silently truncated, never silently widened."""]
    [list
      [item """**Python int is arbitrary-precision.** A Python int
              outside `[-2^63, 2^63-1]` flowing into CX becomes a
              `bigint` on the wire — bindings receive bigint, never
              an error (the decode rule in data-bin §3.4.1)."""]
      [item """**Sized ascriptions are checked at the lexical
              layer.** A literal carrying a `::i8`…`::u64` annotation
              whose value is out of range for that width is a
              CXERLEX-RANGE reject (grammar [55] / lexicon [L25d]) —
              the document never parses."""]
      [item """**Go and Rust have explicit sized types** so the
              host-to-CX direction is unambiguous. CX-to-host
              widening (e.g. CX `::i32` to host `int64`) is silent
              and lossless; a narrowing conversion is the host
              program's own responsibility — the binding hands over
              the declared width."""]
      [item """**Date / datetime precision.** CX preserves nanosecond
              precision. Python `datetime` is microsecond — flowing
              to Python drops sub-microsecond digits. Round-tripping
              a Python `datetime` → CX → host preserves microseconds
              exactly; CX `::datetime` → Python → CX may lose
              precision."""]
      [item """**Bytes and strings.** CX `::string` is always
              valid UTF-8; CX `::bytes` is opaque. Host byte
              sequences that are invalid UTF-8 cannot become a CX
              string — use `::bytes` instead."""]
      [item """**Null vs absent.** CX `null` is a scalar value (a
              present-but-null attribute). Host `None` / `nil` /
              `Option::None` is the absent-value representation. The
              binding distinguishes via the Layer-1 surface: an
              attribute set to `null` returns the host's null-value;
              an absent attribute returns the host's absence-form
              ONLY when called via `Node.attr(name)` — which
              conflates them. To distinguish, inspect
              `Node.attrs()`."""]]

    [body
      """**Type annotation preservation.** Sized variants
         (`::i32`, `::u16`, `::f32`, etc.) round-trip through ast-bin
         and data-bin with their type identifier preserved.
         Bindings translate to the host's native numeric type on
         read and preserve the annotation on write. A Python int that
         enters libcx as `::int` will not silently become `::i32`
         even if it fits; the annotation is sticky."""]]

  [child id=thread-safety n=7.5 title='Thread-safety and concurrency model'
    [intro
      """CX documents are **immutable values**. Sharing a parsed Doc
         across threads is safe by construction — there is no shared
         mutable state to protect. The thread-safety story for each
         binding is mostly about: (a) registering host-spawned
         threads with libcx's GC and (b) wrapping the binding's
         own handle types to match the host's concurrency model."""]

    [child id=ts-doc-immutability n=7.5.1 title='Document immutability'
      [body
        """A parsed `Doc` is logically immutable. `Doc.modify()`
           returns a new Doc; there is no in-place mutation API at any layer. This
           means a single Doc handle can be shared across N threads
           with no synchronization — every thread sees the same
           bytes, every thread's `hash()` returns the same digest,
           every thread's `select_all` returns the same Nodes."""]
      [body
        """Bindings expose this in their type system where they can.
           Rust: `Doc: Send + Sync` is automatic from the underlying
           types. Go: Doc is a value type wrapping a libcx handle —
           safe to pass between goroutines. Python: the GIL
           serializes access in-process; multi-process workers
           pickle the Doc's canonical bytes and reparse. V: Docs
           are shareable across `go` blocks without `shared` /
           `lock` keywords."""]]

    [child id=ts-reentrancy n=7.5.2 title='Layer-1 method reentrancy'
      [body
        """All 16 Layer-1 methods are **reentrant**. Calling
           `doc.select_all(p1)` on thread A while `doc.eval(c1)`
           runs on thread B is safe; neither call mutates anything
           the other reads. The C ABI symbols backing them are
           classified `(S) stateless` in
           [`spec/03-approved/core/abi.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/abi.md) §1.4 — concurrent
           calls on disjoint inputs are safe without external
           synchronization, and concurrent calls on the same input
           buffer are also safe because the buffer is read-only."""]
      [body
        """**Modify** is also reentrant. `doc.modify(...)` does not
           lock the receiver. Two threads modifying the same Doc
           with different focuses each produce their own
           independent new Doc; the original is unchanged. If both
           new Docs need to be combined, do so explicitly with a
           second modify."""]]

    [child id=ts-thread-register n=7.5.3 title='Thread registration (cap bit 26)'
      [body
        """Host-spawned OS threads — Python workers via
           `threading.Thread`, Rust `std::thread::spawn`, Go via
           cgo's per-goroutine OS thread, V via `go fn{}` outside
           the V scheduler — must call `cx_thread_register()`
           before any other `cx_*` call. This registers the
           thread's stack-bottom with libcx's GC."""]
      [body
        """**What each binding actually does differs — know your
           lane.** Rust: every FFI chokepoint calls `ensure_thread()`
           before the C call, and thread exit unregisters — fully
           automatic. Go: each cgo call runs on a locked OS thread
           (`LockOSThread`) and the internal `cxThread()` guard
           registers it — also automatic. **Python does NOT
           register**: the binding assumes the GIL serializes access
           and that libcx is called from a single thread — a
           `threading.Thread` that calls into libcx is unprotected;
           keep libcx calls on one thread in Python. V: threads
           spawned by the V scheduler are already known to the
           GC."""]
      [body
        """Skipping registration is the single most common
           crash-on-thread-spawn bug in user code that bypasses
           the Layer-1 surface and calls the C ABI directly. The
           crash signature is a segfault inside `GC_mark_from`
           during the GC's next collection. If you see that
           backtrace, the first thing to check is whether your
           thread called `cx_thread_register()`."""]]

    [child id=ts-gc-interactions n=7.5.4 title='GC interactions per binding'
      [body
        """The string-in/string-out ABI keeps the memory models
           almost completely decoupled: every string libcx returns is
           copied into a host value at the call boundary and released
           immediately with `cx_free()`, so no host object ever
           points into libcx's Boehm heap between calls."""]
      [list
        [item """**V — Boehm GC throughout.** V's runtime is also
                Boehm-GC. A Doc is a normal V value; no
                bridging needed. V is the simplest case."""]
        [item """**Python — plain refcounted objects.** A Doc is an
                ordinary Python object (the binding-side AST plus the
                source bytes). Python's refcounting reclaims it like
                any other value; libcx holds nothing on its
                behalf."""]
        [item """**Go — plain values.** A Doc is a Go struct owned
                entirely by Go's GC. Each cgo call copies its result
                out of libcx and frees the C string before
                returning — no finalizers, no pinning."""]
        [item """**Rust — plain owned values.** A Doc owns its parsed
                AST and source bytes; dropping it is ordinary Rust
                ownership. The FFI layer copies every returned string
                and calls `cx_free()` inside the wrapper."""]]
      [note
        """`GC_DONT_GC` workaround — the TypeScript binding
           (Node.js + V8) hit a libgc `GC_mark_from` crash that
           necessitated setting `GC_DONT_GC=1`, effectively
           disabling libgc's collector for the process lifetime
           (libcx allocations leak; the process is short-lived
           so it does not matter). This is a per-host workaround,
           not a model — Tier-1 bindings (V/Python/Go/Rust) do
           NOT need it. The TypeScript binding is archived under
           `lang/_archived/typescript/` per
           [[migration]]; restoration depends on root-cause fix
           upstream."""]]]

  [child id=cap-bit-negotiation n=7.6 title='Capability bit negotiation'
    [intro
      """Bindings ship at a specific libcx ABI version but may be
         loaded against an older or newer libcx. The capability
         bitmask returned by `cx_features()` is the runtime contract:
         the binding declares what it needs, libcx declares what it
         supports, and the binding either degrades, errors, or
         proceeds. Per [`spec/03-approved/core/abi.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/abi.md) §3."""]

    [body
      """**The bitmask is a NUL-terminated lowercase hex string**
         encoding a 64-bit mask. Each bit covers one optional
         feature. New features get new bits; bits are never
         repurposed — a retired feature leaves its bit permanently
         RESERVED (bits 8, 13, and 17 are examples). A binding parses
         the hex string to an integer once and tests individual
         bits against it."""]

    [body
      """**Negotiation protocol:**"""]
    [list
      [item """**Binding load.** Binding calls `cx_features()` once,
              caches the result in a process-global variable, and
              compares against its required-bits mask. If a required
              bit is missing, the binding raises a load-time error
              identifying which feature is unavailable."""]
      [item """**Per-call check.** For entry points that may be
              version-gated (e.g. the chunked-table reader requires
              bit 21), the binding's method checks the cached bitmask
              before calling the C ABI and returns a host error early
              when the bit is clear."""]
      [item """**Forward-compat.** A newer libcx with bits set
              that the binding doesn't know about is fine — the
              binding ignores unknown bits. A newer feature is
              accessible only when the binding is itself updated to
              call its entry point."""]
      [item """**Backward-compat.** An older libcx missing bits the
              binding requires is NOT fine — the binding refuses
              to load. There is no fallback path; users must
              upgrade libcx."""]]

    [body
      """**Selected capability bits** (full registry in
         [`spec/03-approved/core/abi.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/abi.md) §3):"""]
    [table
      [tr [th 'bit'] [th 'mask'] [th 'feature']]
      [tr [td '0'] [td '0x1'] [td 'ABI core conversion symbols (always set)']]
      [tr [td '1'] [td '0x2'] [td 'Binary AST (cx_to_ast_bin; always set)']]
      [tr [td '5'] [td '0x20'] [td 'data_bin one-shot loaders / dumpers']]
      [tr [td '15'] [td '0x8000'] [td 'Logfmt mode (top-level bare attributes)']]
      [tr [td '16'] [td '0x10000'] [td 'Numeric underscores (1_000_000)']]
      [tr [td '17'] [td '0x20000'] [td 'RESERVED — superseded by bit 23 (query bit 23 for Arrow)']]
      [tr [td '21'] [td '0x200000'] [td 'Chunked-table format + streaming table reader/writer ABI']]
      [tr [td '23'] [td '0x800000'] [td 'Apache Arrow C-Data interop (libcx_arrow)']]
      [tr [td '24'] [td '0x1000000'] [td 'Schema-driven data-bin encoding']]
      [tr [td '26'] [td '0x4000000'] [td 'Thread-init handshake (cx_init / cx_thread_register)']]
      [tr [td '28'] [td '0x10000000'] [td 'cx_code_eval family — unified evaluator entry']]
      [tr [td '29'] [td '0x20000000'] [td 'Collection literals — array / map / sequence Item kinds']]
      [tr [td '30'] [td '0x40000000'] [td 'First-class function values ([?fn])']]
      [tr [td '31'] [td '0x80000000'] [td 'Code-to-diagram (cx_code_diagram)']]
      [tr [td '32'] [td '0x100000000'] [td 'Code tree projection (cx_code_tree)']]]

    [example lang=python
      """import cxlib as cx

         # cx.features() parses the hex string to an int bitmask.
         caps = cx.features()

         if not (caps & (1 << 23)):
             # No Arrow C-Data interop in this libcx — stay on the
             # text/data-bin surfaces instead of the Arrow bridge.
             use_arrow = False
         else:
             use_arrow = True"""]]

  [child id=ffi-lifetime n=7.7 title='FFI lifetime and memory ownership'
    [intro
      """The string ABI keeps ownership simple: libcx allocates a
         result string on its own heap, the binding copies it into a
         host value, and the wrapper calls `cx_free()` before
         returning. A `Doc` is an ordinary host object — the
         binding-side AST plus the retained source bytes — owned
         entirely by the host's memory model. Only the streaming
         handle families carry an explicit lifecycle."""]

    [child id=lifetime-v n=7.7.1 title='V — Boehm GC end-to-end'
      [body
        """V uses Boehm GC at the language level; the V binding
           imports the core directly, so a Doc is a normal V value
           that becomes collectable when no V variable references it.
           No explicit close exists or is needed."""]]

    [child id=lifetime-python n=7.7.2 title='Python — plain refcounted objects'
      [body
        """A Python `Doc` is an ordinary object; Python's refcounting
           reclaims it deterministically when the last reference goes
           out of scope. There is no `close()`, no `__del__`, and no
           context-manager protocol on `Doc` — there is nothing to
           release, because every libcx string was copied and freed
           at the call boundary."""]
      [body
        """The classes that DO carry a `close()` and context-manager
           protocol are the handle-backed ones: `Stream`,
           `TableReader`, `TableWriter`, and `EventWriter`. Use
           `with` on those."""]]

    [child id=lifetime-go n=7.7.3 title='Go — plain values'
      [body
        """A Go `Doc` is a value type owned by Go's GC; there is no
           `Close()`, no `runtime.SetFinalizer`, and no
           closed-Doc error state. Each cgo call copies its result
           out of the C string and frees it before returning. The
           handle-backed stream and table types are the only ones
           with explicit close methods."""]]

    [child id=lifetime-rust n=7.7.4 title='Rust — plain owned values'
      [body
        """A Rust `Doc` owns its parsed AST and source bytes;
           dropping it is ordinary ownership, with no custom `Drop`
           impl needed. The FFI layer copies every returned string
           and calls `cx_free()` inside the wrapper, so no Rust value
           ever points into libcx's heap."""]]

    [child id=lifetime-node-borrowing n=7.7.5 title='Nodes are plain values too'
      [body
        """Nodes returned from a Doc's selection methods are host
           values built from the binding-side AST. They do not borrow
           libcx memory, and there is no handle-after-free failure
           mode — a Node outliving its Doc is just a host object
           outliving another host object."""]
      [body
        """**Important — Nodes are NOT mutable shared state.** Two
           Nodes derived from the same Doc do not interfere with
           each other. The Doc is logically immutable ([[modify]]
           produces a NEW Doc; the original is unchanged). A Node is
           a read-only window onto a portion of the Doc."""]]

    [child id=lifetime-explicit-release n=7.7.6 title='Explicit release — handles only'
      [body
        """The only explicit lifecycles at the ABI are the handle
           families: `cx_events_close` for streaming events,
           `cx_table_reader_close` / `cx_table_writer_close` for
           chunked tables, and `cx_events_writer_close` for the
           streaming writer. Bindings surface these as `close()`
           methods (context managers in Python); everything else is
           ordinary host memory."""]
      [body
        """For document-streaming workloads, prefer the streaming
           surfaces (see [[streaming-data]]) over parsing whole
           documents; streaming bounds resident memory to one chunk
           regardless of total input size."""]]]

  [child id=schema-codegen n=7.8 title='Schema-driven codegen'
    [intro
      """When a CX schema (`.cxs`, see [[schema]]) is available,
         every binding could generate typed Layer-2 projections from
         it. This is **planned follow-up work**; the spec slot
         is reserved in [`spec/03-approved/misc/bindings.md`](../../spec/03-approved/misc/bindings.md)
         §7, and no CLI surface for it exists yet — not even a
         stub."""]

    [example lang=cx
      """[# users.cxs — the schema source #]
         [schema
           [element name=user
             [attribute name=id    type=int    required=true]
             [attribute name=email type=string required=true]
             [attribute name=active type=bool  default=true]]]"""]

    [body
      """**Planned per-target output (future revision):**"""]
    [list
      [item """**Python** — a generated module with a `User`
              dataclass, type-checked accessors, and a round-trip
              `users_from_doc(doc) -> list[User]` /
              `doc_from_users(users) -> Doc` pair."""]
      [item """**Go** — generated structs and methods,
              `go fmt`-clean."""]
      [item """**Rust** — generated structs and typed
              materialization helpers, `rustfmt`-clean."""]
      [item """**V** — no codegen target; V binding's Layer-1
              surface already gives typed access to schema fields
              when the schema is loaded at runtime."""]]

    [body
      """**Status — the schema validator is available but codegen
         does not exist yet, even as a stub.** There is no codegen
         verb on the CLI: the shipped `cx schema` family is
         `cx schema infer` (synthesize a `.cxs` from a corpus) and
         `cx schema export --to=json-schema` (project a schema to
         JSON Schema 2020-12). Typed projections from a schema are
         design work that has not landed in any binding."""]]

  [child id=install-hello n=7.9 title='Install + hello world per binding'
    [intro
      """The minimum-viable per-binding workflow: install, parse a
         tiny document, compute a hash, evaluate one expression. Every
         binding's hello-world produces byte-identical hash output;
         that is the simplest cross-binding parity check."""]

    [child id=install-v n=7.9.1 title='V (native reference)'
      [body
        """V is the native language of libcx. The binding lives in
           `lang/v/native/`. Importing it is a normal V
           module import; no FFI involved. The pure-V package is
           published as module `cx` — install with
           `v install --git https://github.com/cx-home/cx-v`."""]
      [example lang=v
        """// v install --git https://github.com/cx-home/cx-v
           import cx

           fn main() {
             doc := cx.parse_doc('[hello world=true]')!
             println(doc.hash()!)
             // -> 8f3d... (64-hex-char SHA-256)
             v := doc.eval('//hello/@world')!
             println(v) // -> true
           }"""]]

    [child id=install-python n=7.9.2 title='Python'
      [body
        """`pip install cxlib` installs the Python binding (when
           published — pre-publish use `pip install -e
           lang/python/`). Requires Python 3.10+."""]
      [example lang=python
        """# pip install cxlib
           from cxlib import Doc

           doc = Doc.parse(b"[hello world=true]")
           print(doc.hash())
           # -> 8f3d... (matches V output exactly)
           print(doc.eval("//hello/@world"))
           # -> true"""]]

    [child id=install-go n=7.9.3 title='Go'
      [body
        """`go get github.com/cx-home/cx/lang/go` installs the Go
           module; the package is
           `github.com/cx-home/cx/lang/go/cxlib`. Requires Go
           1.22+."""]
      [example lang=go
        """// go get github.com/cx-home/cx/lang/go
           package main

           import (
             "fmt"
             "github.com/cx-home/cx/lang/go/cxlib"
           )

           func main() {
             doc, _ := cxlib.ParseDoc([]byte("[hello world=true]"))
             h, _ := doc.Hash()
             fmt.Println(h)
             // -> 8f3d... (matches V + Python)
             v, _ := doc.Eval("//hello/@world")
             fmt.Println(v) // -> true
           }"""]]

    [child id=install-rust n=7.9.4 title='Rust'
      [body
        """`cargo add cxlib` adds the Rust binding (when published
           — pre-publish use a path dependency to
           `lang/rust/cxlib/`). Requires Rust 1.75+."""]
      [example lang=rust
        """// Cargo.toml: cxlib = "..."   [; see crates.io for the current version ]
           use cxlib::code::Doc;

           fn main() -> Result<(), String> {
             let doc = Doc::parse(b"[hello world=true]")?;
             println!("{}", doc.hash()?);
             // -> 8f3d... (matches V + Python + Go)
             let v = doc.eval("//hello/@world")?;
             println!("{}", v); // -> true
             Ok(())
           }"""]]

    [child id=install-verify n=7.9.5 title='Verify cross-binding parity'
      [body
        """The fastest cross-binding sanity check is to hash the
           same input from the CLI and from each binding and confirm
           all five outputs match:"""]
      [example lang=text
        """$ echo '[hello world=true]' | cx hash
           8f3d2a1b...
           $ v run hello_v.v        # -> 8f3d2a1b...
           $ python hello.py        # -> 8f3d2a1b...
           $ go run hello.go        # -> 8f3d2a1b...
           $ cargo run --bin hello  # -> 8f3d2a1b..."""]
      [body
        """If any binding produces a different hash, that binding
           has drifted from the canonical-bytes contract — file
           against [`spec/03-approved/misc/parity-matrix.md`](../../spec/03-approved/misc/parity-matrix.md)
           and the conformance harness will catch it on next
           CI run."""]]]

  [child id=quickstarts n=7.10 title='Quickstarts (V / Python / Go / Rust)'
    [intro
      """Beyond hello-world, each Tier-1 binding has an idiomatic
         pattern for the most common CX workloads: parse, select,
         iterate, modify, serialize. These quickstarts show the
         Layer-2 idiomatic path; the Layer-1 fallback is always
         available."""]

    [child id=v n=7.10.1 title='V (native reference)'
      [body
        """V is the native language of libcx. The Layer-1 surface IS
           V's idiomatic API; errors bubble via `!` / `?`
           propagation. Concurrency uses `go fn(){}` with channels;
           Docs are safely shareable between goroutines without
           `shared` / `lock` keywords because they are immutable
           values."""]
      [example lang=v
        """import cx
           import os

           fn main() {
             data := os.read_file('users.cx')!
             doc := cx.parse_doc(data)!
             for user in doc.select_all('//user[= $_@active true]')! {
               println(user.attr('email') or { '<no email>' })
             }
             new_doc := doc.modify('//user[= $_@id 1]/@name',
                                   "[set 'Alice']")!
             os.write_file('users-updated.cx', new_doc.bytes())!
             println(new_doc.hash()!)
           }"""]]

    [child id=python n=7.10.2 title='Python'
      [body
        """Python uses the `cxlib` package; the ctypes FFI bridge is
           in `lang/python/cxlib/cx.py`. Layer 2 sugar lives in
           `cxlib.idioms`. Requires Python 3.10+. Typed projections
           from a `.cxs` schema are future work (see
           [[schema-codegen]])."""]
      [example lang=python
        """from cxlib.idioms import Doc    # opt-in sugar

           with open('users.cx', 'rb') as f:
               doc = Doc.parse(f.read())

           # Division-iterator over a path
           emails = [n for n in doc / "//user[= $_@active true]/@email"]

           # Subscript-modify
           new_doc = doc.clone()
           new_doc["//user[= $_@id 1]/@name"] = 'Alice'

           with open('users-updated.cx', 'wb') as f:
               f.write(new_doc.bytes())
           print(new_doc.hash())"""]]

    [child id=go n=7.10.3 title='Go'
      [body
        """Go uses cgo to call the C ABI. The module is
           `github.com/cx-home/cx/lang/go`; Layer 1 and the Layer-2
           builders share the one `cxlib` package. Requires Go
           1.22+. The cgo bridge finds libcx through the repo's
           Makefile lanes; `LIBCX_PATH` / `LIBCX_LIB_DIR` point it
           at a specific build when needed. Docs are safe to share
           across goroutines — each cgo call runs on a locked,
           registered OS thread."""]
      [example lang=go
        """package main

           import (
             "fmt"; "os"
             "github.com/cx-home/cx/lang/go/cxlib"
           )

           func main() {
             data, _ := os.ReadFile("users.cx")
             doc, _ := cxlib.ParseDoc(data)
             emails, _ := doc.Filter("user").Where("= $_@active true").Get("/@email")
             for _, e := range emails { fmt.Println(e.Body()) }
             action, _ := cxlib.Set("Alice")
             newDoc, _ := doc.Modify("//user[= $_@id 1]/@name", action)
             h, _ := newDoc.Hash()
             fmt.Println(h)
           }"""]]

    [child id=rust n=7.10.4 title='Rust'
      [body
        """Rust wraps the C ABI through hand-written `extern "C"`
           declarations (no bindgen); the crate lives at
           `lang/rust/cxlib/`. Layer 2 in `cxlib::idioms`. Requires
           Rust 1.75+. A `Doc` is a plain owned value; share it
           across threads with `Arc<Doc>` — every FFI chokepoint
           registers its calling thread automatically."""]
      [example lang=rust
        """use cxlib::code::Doc;
           use cxlib::idioms::DocExt;
           use std::fs;

           fn main() -> Result<(), String> {
             let data = fs::read("users.cx").map_err(|e| e.to_string())?;
             let doc = Doc::parse(&data)?;
             for email in doc.select_iter("//user[= $_@active true]/@email")? {
               println!("{}", email.name());
             }
             let new_doc = doc.modify("//user[= $_@id 1]/@name", "[set 'Alice']")?;
             println!("{}", new_doc.hash()?);

             // Concurrent reads — Doc is plain owned data
             use std::{thread, sync::Arc};
             let shared = Arc::new(doc);
             let handles: Vec<_> = (0..4).map(|_| {
               let d = Arc::clone(&shared);
               thread::spawn(move || d.select_all("//user").unwrap().len())
             }).collect();
             for h in handles { println!("{}", h.join().unwrap()); }
             Ok(())
           }"""]]]

  [# Migrated from docs-src/content/bindings/*.cx during the
     Phase B docs → guide consolidation (2026-05-24). Per-binding
     reference detail: install command, Layer-1 method list,
     atom example, type-mapping, threading note. Kept as a
     consolidated child so the section's primary structure
     (§7.1–§7.10) stays the reading spine. #]

  [child id=binding-detail n=7.11 title='Per-binding reference snippets'
    [intro
      """Compact reference for each Tier-1 binding: install command,
         the 16-method Layer-1 surface in idiomatic form, the atom
         scalar kind, schema validation, and the
         type-mapping table. The full normative contract is
         [`spec/03-approved/misc/bindings.md §2.1`](../../spec/03-approved/misc/bindings.md); the C ABI
         is [`spec/03-approved/core/abi.md`](../../spec/03-approved/core/abi.md)."""]

    [child id=c-abi-detail n=7.11.1 title='C ABI — symbol catalog'
      [body
        """The libcx C ABI partitions into nine families:
           format conversion, canonical+identity, binary forms
           (ast_bin / data_bin), evaluation (`cx_code_eval*`),
           diagram+tree, schema validation, identity
           and resolution (id / IDREF), streaming events + tables,
           and threading + memory. Capability bits gate each
           family; bindings query `cx_features()` at load time and
           degrade or refuse to load when a required bit is clear.
           Every `char *` returned by libcx must be released with
           `cx_free` — never `free()`."""]
      [example lang=c
        """/* Format conversion. */
           char *cx_to_cx        (const char *in, char **err);
           char *cx_to_xml       (const char *in, char **err);
           char *cx_to_json      (const char *in, char **err);
           char *cx_to_yaml      (const char *in, char **err);
           char *cx_to_toml      (const char *in, char **err);
           char *cx_to_md        (const char *in, char **err);
           char *cx_to_csv       (const char *in, char **err);
           char *cx_from_xml     (const char *in, char **err);
           /* …yaml / toml / md / csv variants… */

           /* Canonical + identity (cap bit 7). */
           char *cx_canonical(const char *in, char **err);
           char *cx_hash     (const char *in, char **err);
           int   cx_eq       (const char *a, const char *b, char **err);
           char *cx_diff     (const char *a, const char *b, const char *fmt, char **err);
           char *cx_lint     (const char *in, const char *fmt, const char *disabled, char **err);

           /* Binary forms. */
           char *cx_to_ast_bin    (const char *in, char **err);
           char *cx_ast_bin_to_cx (const char *ast_bin, char **err);
           char *cx_to_data_bin   (const char *in, char **err);
           char *cx_from_data_bin (const char *data_bin, char **err);

           /* Evaluation (cap bit 28; renamed from cx_program_eval). */
           char *cx_code_eval         (const char *input, const char *program,
                                       const char *output_target, char **err);
           char *cx_code_eval_with_len(const char *input,   size_t input_len,
                                       const char *program, size_t program_len,
                                       const char *output_target, char **err);
           char *cx_code_eval_streaming(const char *input,   size_t input_len,
                                        const char *program, size_t program_len,
                                        const char *output_target,
                                        cx_code_write_cb write_cb, void *user,
                                        char **err);

           /* Diagram + tree (cap bits 31 / 32). */
           char *cx_code_diagram (const char *source, size_t source_len,
                                  const char *format, size_t format_len);
           char *cx_code_tree    (const char *source, size_t source_len,
                                  size_t *out_len);
           char *cx_code_ast_json(const char *source, size_t source_len);

           /* Schema validation (cap bit 25). */
           char *cx_validate                (const char *doc, const char *schema, char **err);
           char *cx_validate_apply_defaults (const char *doc, const char *schema,
                                             char **modified_doc_out, char **err);

           /* Threading + memory (cap bit 26). */
           int  cx_init(void);
           int  cx_thread_register(void);
           int  cx_thread_unregister(void);
           void cx_free(char *p);

           /* Capability + version queries. */
           char *cx_features(void);     /* lowercase hex of 64-bit bitmask */
           char *cx_abi_version(void);  /* ABI semver, e.g. "2.0" */
           char *cx_version(void);      /* library semver (repo VERSION) */

           /* Include-root variants (Phase 3.6). */
           char *cx_to_cx_with_include_root      (const char *in, const char *root, char **err);
           char *cx_to_ast_bin_with_include_root (const char *in, const char *root, char **err);
           char *cx_to_data_bin_with_include_root(const char *in, const char *root, char **err);

           /* Wasm arena tuning (wasm build only). */
           int cx_wasm_set_arena_size(unsigned int bytes);
           int cx_wasm_reset(void);"""]
      [note
        """v0.8.0 adds bits 31 (`cx_code_diagram`), 32 (`cx_code_tree`), 33 (atoms), 34 (`[?def]`), 35 (`[?lib]`), and 36 (PathNode wire format / ast_bin v8). Bit 28 (CX code evaluator) is unchanged from v0.7.6."""]] [; version-literal-ok ]

    [child id=v-detail n=7.11.2 title='V binding — install, Layer 1, atom'
      [body
        """V is the native reference implementation. libcx is
           written in V; the V binding under `lang/v/native/`
           imports the `cx` package directly with zero FFI overhead.
           Every other Tier-1 binding wraps the same C ABI that the
           V core exports. The `lang/v/cffi/` variant was archived
           to `lang/_archived/v-cffi/`; preserved as a potential FFI-overhead
           benchmark."""]
      [example lang=shell """v install --git https://github.com/cx-home/cx-v"""]
      [example lang=v
        """import cx

           // Doc construction + identity (Layer 1 — 16 methods).
           doc := cx.parse_doc(bytes) or { panic(err) }
           out := doc.bytes()
           h   := doc.hash()!
           eq  := doc.equals(other)!

           // Evaluation (wraps cx_code_eval).
           val := doc.eval(code) or { panic(err) }

           // CXPath selection.
           nodes := doc.select_all('//user[= $_@active true]/@email')!
           first := doc.select('//user[= $_@id 1]')!

           // Pure-functional update — the action is a CX program.
           new_doc := doc.modify('//user[= $_@id 1]/@name', "[set 'Alice']")!

           // Convenience + root.
           users := doc.find_all('user')
           root  := doc.root()?

           // Node accessors.
           name     := node.name()
           val_opt  := node.attr('email')
           attrs    := node.attrs()
           children := node.children()
           body     := node.body()
           kind     := node.kind()

           // Includes — explicit include root on the conversion surface.
           resolved := cx.to_cx_with_include_root(input, '/proj') or { panic(err) }

           // Atom scalar — type-strict, no string coercion.
           a := cx.atom('paid')!
           assert cx.is_atom(a)
           assert cx.atom_name(a)! == 'paid'"""]
      [note
        """The V core exports `cx_code_diagram` (auto-detected
           `erDiagram` or `flowchart` Mermaid output) and
           `cx_code_tree` (JSON projection with source `loc` ranges
           for the playground tree pane). These exports power the
           wasm playground and are not wrapped by the FFI bindings;
           cap bit 31 (diagram) and bit 32 (tree) advertise
           availability."""]]

    [child id=python-detail n=7.11.3 title='Python binding — install, Layer 1, type map'
      [body
        """The Python binding wraps the libcx C ABI through ctypes
           (`lang/python/cxlib/cx.py`). Layer 1 method names are
           snake_case per Python convention; semantics are identical
           to V / Go / Rust. Layer 2 idiomatic sugar —
           subscript-with-CXPath, division iteration — lives in the
           opt-in `cxlib.idioms` module and desugars deterministically
           to Layer 1 calls."""]
      [example lang=shell
        """pip install cxlib
           # Or from source (pre-publish):
           git clone https://github.com/cx-home/cx
           cd cx/lang/python
           pip install -e ."""]
      [example lang=python
        """import cxlib
           from cxlib import Doc

           # Doc construction + identity.
           doc = Doc.parse(open('users.cx', 'rb').read())
           out = doc.bytes()
           h   = doc.hash()
           eq  = doc.equals(other)

           # Evaluation (cx_code_eval).
           val = doc.eval(code)

           # CXPath.
           emails = doc.select_all('//user[= $_@active true]/@email')
           first  = doc.select('//user[= $_@id 1]')

           # Pure-functional update — the action is a CX program string.
           new_doc = doc.modify('//user[= $_@id 1]/@name', "[set 'Alice']")
           new_doc = doc.modify('//user[= $_@banned true]', '[delete]')

           # Node access.
           shop = Doc.parse(b'[shop name=Pepe [pizza name=Margherita price=12]]').root()
           print(shop.attr('name'))              # 'Pepe'
           print(shop.children()[0].attr('name')) # 'Margherita'

           # Atom — type-strict, no string coercion.
           a = cxlib.atom('paid')
           assert cxlib.is_atom(a)
           assert cxlib.atom_name(a) == 'paid'
           assert a != 'paid'

           # Streaming — stream() takes the CX text.
           with open('events.cx') as f:
               for event in cxlib.stream(f.read()):
                   if event.type == 'StartElement' and event.name == 'error':
                       print(event.attrs)

           # Schema validation.
           report = cxlib.validate(
               open('order.cx').read(),
               open('order.cxs').read(),
           )"""]
      [note
        """Type mapping (CX → Python): `int → int`, `bigint → int`,
           `decimal → decimal.Decimal`, `float / f32 / f64 → float`,
           `bool → bool`, `string → str`, `atom → cxlib.Atom`,
           `date → datetime.date`, `datetime →
           datetime.datetime`, `bytes → bytes`, `null → None`."""]]

    [child id=go-detail n=7.11.4 title='Go binding — install, Layer 1, cgo'
      [body
        """The Go binding wraps the libcx C ABI through cgo. Layer 1
           method names use Go PascalCase per host convention;
           semantics are identical to V / Python / Rust. Each
           goroutine that calls into libcx gets a thread
           registration on first use; the binding manages the Boehm
           GC handshake at every FFI chokepoint, so calling code
           does not need to think about it. Stateless format
           conversions are safe to call concurrently on disjoint
           inputs."""]
      [example lang=shell """go get github.com/cx-home/cx/lang/go"""]
      [example lang=go
        """import "github.com/cx-home/cx/lang/go/cxlib"

           // Doc construction + identity.
           doc, err := cxlib.ParseDoc(input)
           out     := doc.Bytes()
           h, _    := doc.Hash()
           eq, _   := doc.Equals(other)

           // Evaluation (cx_code_eval).
           val, err := doc.Eval(code)

           // CXPath.
           emails, _ := doc.SelectAll("//user[= $_@active true]/@email")
           first, _  := doc.Select("//user[= $_@id 1]")

           // Pure-functional update — Set builds the action program.
           action, _ := cxlib.Set("Alice")
           newDoc, _ := doc.Modify("//user[= $_@id 1]/@name", action)

           // Tree access.
           tree, _ := cxlib.ParseDoc([]byte("[shop [pizza name=Margherita price=12]]"))
           pizza := tree.FindAll("pizza")[0]
           name  := pizza.Attr("name")
           price := pizza.Attr("price")

           // Atom — type-strict, no string coercion.
           a, _ := cxlib.Atom("paid")
           fmt.Println(cxlib.IsAtom(a))    // true
           fmt.Println(a.AtomName())       // "paid"

           // Schema validation — doc + schema as CX text.
           report, _ := cxlib.Validate(docText, schemaText)

           // Streaming — Stream returns the event slice.
           events, _ := cxlib.Stream(cxStr)
           for _, e := range events {
               if e.Type == "StartElement" && e.Name == "error" {
                   fmt.Println(e.Attrs)
               }
           }"""]]

    [child id=rust-detail n=7.11.5 title='Rust binding — install, Layer 1, threading'
      [body
        """The Rust binding wraps the libcx C ABI through
           hand-written `extern "C"` declarations. Layer 1 method
           names are snake_case per Rust convention. The binding
           registers each calling thread with libcx via
           `ensure_thread` at every FFI chokepoint;
           `cx_thread_register` and `cx_thread_unregister` fire
           automatically, so manual setup is unnecessary. Stateless
           conversions can be called from any thread without
           coordination. Schema validate IS implemented for Rust —
           the 📋 in `spec/03-approved/misc/parity-matrix.md` §2 is a
           stale row awaiting its update."""]
      [example lang=shell """cargo add cxlib"""]
      [example lang=rust
        """use cxlib::code::{parse, Doc, Node};

           // Doc construction + identity.
           let doc: Doc = parse(&input)?;
           let out: Vec<u8> = doc.bytes();
           let h: String    = doc.hash()?;
           let eq: bool     = doc.equals(&other)?;

           // Evaluation (cx_code_eval).
           let val = doc.eval(code)?;

           // CXPath.
           let emails: Vec<Node> = doc.select_all("//user[= $_@active true]/@email")?;
           let first: Option<Node> = doc.select("//user[= $_@id 1]")?;

           // Pure-functional update — the action is a CX program.
           let new_doc = doc.modify("//user[= $_@id 1]/@name", "[set 'Alice']")?;

           // Atom.
           let a = cxlib::Atom::new("paid")?;
           assert_eq!(a.name(), "paid");

           // Schema validation — two-arg, returns a ValidationReport.
           let report = cxlib::validate::validate(doc_text, schema_text)?;"""]
      [note
        """Type mapping (CX → Rust): `int → i64` (or sized variant
           if `::iN`/`::uN`), `bigint → num_bigint::BigInt`, `decimal
           → bigdecimal::BigDecimal` (arbitrary precision — not
           `rust_decimal`, whose 96-bit form cannot represent the
           wire image), `float → f64` (or `f32` if
           sized), `bool → bool`, `string → String`, `atom →
           cxlib::Atom`, `date →
           chrono::NaiveDate`, `datetime → chrono::DateTime`,
           `bytes → Vec<u8>`."""]]]

  [child id=abi-stability n=7.12 title='ABI stability and versioning'
    [intro
      """The C ABI is the contract that lets every binding call into
         libcx without recompilation, so its evolution rules are worth
         stating plainly. The normative policy lives in
         [`spec/03-approved/process/governance.md`](../../spec/03-approved/process/governance.md); this is the
         practicing summary."""]

    [list
      [item """**Semver discipline, pre-1.0.** On the 0.x line the ABI
              may break at minor versions; every break is annotated in
              `CHANGELOG.md` with a migration note. From 1.0 the ABI
              breaks only at major versions, with a published migration
              guide."""]
      [item """**Capability bits are the forward-compatibility
              mechanism.** A binding examines the feature bitmask at
              load and enables, degrades, or refuses per feature — the
              negotiation flow and the current bit map are in the
              capability-bit subsection above. A bit, once assigned,
              keeps its meaning forever; features never reuse bits."""]
      [item """**Newer libcx, older binding: works.** The library
              exports a superset; unused symbols and unclaimed bits sit
              idle. This is the normal state after a library upgrade —
              bindings keep working without recompile."""]
      [item """**Older libcx, newer binding: refuses or degrades,
              never limps.** A missing required bit fails the load with
              a clear error; a missing optional bit disables that API
              surface, which then errors as \"feature unavailable\"
              when called — never a silent wrong answer."""]
      [item """**Symbols are append-only.** Frozen signatures stay
              frozen; a signature that must change arrives as a new
              suffixed symbol beside the old one, and removal is a
              major-version event."""]]

    [body
      """Schema compatibility versions independently of the ABI — what
         may change within a schema line, and how breaking schema
         changes travel, is Ring 0 material: [[schema-evolution]]."""]]

  [child id=wasm-story n=7.13 title='The WebAssembly build'
    [intro
      """libcx also builds to WebAssembly (`scripts/wasm/
         build_libcx_wasm.sh`; artifacts land in `dist/wasm/`). The
         wasm build is a **strict subset of the native C ABI** — every
         exported symbol already exists in the native library — so
         tools written against the ABI work identically against wasm
         for the surface it exposes, through the same JS wrapper the
         playground uses (`scripts/wasm/cxlib.js`)."""]

    [body
      """**What wasm is for.** Browser-side tooling — the playground,
         in-page validators and formatters, documentation that
         evaluates its own examples — and low-trust embedding, where
         the wasm sandbox is a hard isolation boundary: the embedded
         engine cannot reach the host filesystem or network at all,
         however the capability set is misconfigured. The playground
         and its delivery modes (single-threaded static hosting versus
         the locally-served build where `[par]` gets real threads) are
         covered with the rest of the tooling in [[tooling]]."""]

    [body
      """**What wasm is not.** The production target. Native
         deployments use the CLI, the language bindings, or libcx as a
         shared library — none of which carry the wasm build's
         event-loop instrumentation, bundle-size pressure, or
         single-thread default. Expect wasm to run at a substantial
         fraction of native speed and treat the playground as an
         explorable demonstration surface, not a benchmark."""]

    [note
      """A pure-JavaScript reimplementation of CX (no wasm) is a
         long-term option that is deliberately **not** scheduled: it
         would mean two implementations held in parity with the spec.
         The wasm path keeps a single source of truth — one engine,
         compiled twice."""]]]
